using System.Net;

namespace ShiftSoftware.ADP.Rastgo;

/// <summary>
/// The chrome <see cref="DashboardRenderer"/> and <see cref="TrendsRenderer"/> share: the compiled
/// stylesheet, the <c>&lt;head&gt;</c> that inlines it, the brand lockup, and the JavaScript that is
/// identical on both pages (floating tooltips, the theme toggle, the fold primitive). The two renderers
/// share no markup — only this.
/// <para>
/// Both pages ship as ONE self-contained HTML file with no external assets, because they have to work
/// opened straight off a file share. So the stylesheet is inlined rather than linked, and there is no
/// charting library, no icon font and no CDN anywhere on either page.
/// </para>
/// </summary>
internal static class PageChrome
{
    /// <summary>
    /// The pages' stylesheet, compiled and minified rather than hand-written.
    /// <para>
    /// Source is the ADP design language — the daisyUI 5 "harness" theme whose tokens live in
    /// <c>ADP.WebComponents/adp-web-components/src/templates/harness.src.css</c> — built by Tailwind 4 +
    /// daisyUI 5 against a copy of that theme block, plus two Rastgo-only additions the components do not
    /// need: the <c>--series-*</c> line palette and the <c>.cell-norun</c> hatch.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// <b>Regenerating:</b> Tailwind emits only the utilities it can literally SEE in the markup it is
    /// pointed at, so the class names in both renderers must be literal strings. A class composed at
    /// runtime — <c>"bg-" + token</c> — silently does not exist in this blob. That is why the status →
    /// colour maps in both renderers are written out in full rather than built from the enum name.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    public const string Css = """
